WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:00.780
CORNELIUS WILLIS: Hi.

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:01.705
I'm Cornelius Willis.

00:00:01.705 --> 00:00:03.561
I'm with the Cloud
Platform team at Google.

00:00:03.561 --> 00:00:06.060
And today we're going to have
a conversation about container

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:06.560
technology.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:08.910
In fact, you might say
a grown up conversation.

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:12.120
And we've got some leaders from
the container software industry

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:14.130
here to share
perspectives with us.

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:18.330
Starting with Alex Polvi,
who is the CEO of CoreOS.

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.830
We also have Craig McLuckie who
leads the product management

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:23.470
team that works on
containers here at Google.

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:27.260
And Kit Colbert, who's the
CTO for the cloud-native

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.085
applications effort at VMware.

00:00:29.085 --> 00:00:30.460
And with that,
we'll get started.

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:32.107
We have questions
for all of you.

00:00:32.107 --> 00:00:33.190
CRAIG MCLUCKIE: All right.

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:34.648
CORNELIUS WILLIS:
So, first of all,

00:00:34.648 --> 00:00:36.717
why does anybody care
about container technology?

00:00:36.717 --> 00:00:38.425
Why are we even having
this conversation?

00:00:38.425 --> 00:00:39.264
Why does it matter?

00:00:39.264 --> 00:00:41.180
KIT COLBERT: You want
to take that one, Craig?

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:42.888
CRAIG MCLUCKIE: Maybe
I'll take this one.

00:00:42.888 --> 00:00:45.200
So I think about
container technologies

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.090
as being important
for two reasons.

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:48.867
The first reason
is, what is today.

00:00:48.867 --> 00:00:51.450
And then second part of it is,
where's it going in the future,

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:51.950
right?

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:53.990
And both of these
are worth discussing.

00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:56.840
The first part of it, today,
as exemplified by Docker,

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:59.270
is that container technologies
have solved a really hard

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:02.270
problem around the hermetic
sealing and deployment

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:03.020
of an application.

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:05.379
So they've taken something
that has historically

00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:08.700
been very fraught, and
difficult, and occasionally

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:11.052
fails to becoming something
that's very predictable

00:01:11.052 --> 00:01:13.010
and has a great developer
experience around it.

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:14.240
And so they've solved
this hard problem

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.110
in a way that's
accessible to engineers.

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:17.752
And engineers like
it for that reason.

00:01:17.752 --> 00:01:19.710
The second thing I think
a lot about containers

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:21.750
is, where containers are going.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:23.590
And if you think about
it, containers really

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:27.422
grew up as a technology
in the internet giants

00:01:27.422 --> 00:01:28.880
because it's the
only practical way

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:31.260
to accomplish
internet-scale computing.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:33.770
Now as we look to the
future, every enterprise

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:37.480
is on the cusp of needing to
act as internet company, as IoT,

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:39.940
and mobile-enabled
workforces emerge.

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:41.910
They need access
to the capabilities

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:43.890
that have previously
been the providence

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:45.660
of the internet giants alone.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:47.812
And what I see containers
technologies doing

00:01:47.812 --> 00:01:49.270
is really moving
the world forwards

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:51.940
into this cloud-native
computing paradigm,

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:55.280
and letting everybody benefit
from the same paradigms

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:59.100
of computing that people like
Google, Facebook, or Twitter,

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:01.630
or others have had to sort
of build for themselves

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:02.830
and pioneer for themselves.

00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:04.900
CORNELIUS WILLIS: So if
we think about a world,

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:07.120
before and after, right?

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:09.165
What is container
technology replacing?

00:02:09.165 --> 00:02:12.051
And what will
[INAUDIBLE] replace?

00:02:12.051 --> 00:02:13.800
What are we doing now
today that we're not

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.160
going to do in the future, when
everything running containers?

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:17.660
KIT COLBERT: Yeah,
I mean, one thing

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:19.070
I wanted to add
to Craig's point,

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:20.620
actually, on the
previous question,

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:22.565
was that I think a lot of times
when we use the word container,

00:02:22.565 --> 00:02:24.856
we actually bring in a lot
of other concepts with that.

00:02:24.856 --> 00:02:25.740
Right?

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:27.800
There's one thing
around basically what

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:29.750
container technologies
like Docker have done,

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:31.940
which is provide like a
great packaging framework

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:33.780
and then the Docker
engine orchestration

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:36.260
framework on the Linux host.

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:38.610
But there's a lot of other
aspects to the application,

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:40.685
and the way the application
that is produced,

00:02:40.685 --> 00:02:43.780
that I think really derive like
the kind of end-to-end benefits

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:45.510
that folks are you looking for.

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:47.295
So containers are
absolutely enabling,

00:02:47.295 --> 00:02:48.420
and they're awesome, right?

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:49.170
And they're great.

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:52.540
But then there's questions like
the application architecture.

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:54.560
And so a lot of that is
breaking down these more

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:56.140
monolithic
architected apps to be

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:58.700
something you might call
distributed or micro services.

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:01.360
And as you do that, you
enable your development teams

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:03.470
to start moving
more independently,

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:04.907
build more agility, into that.

00:03:04.907 --> 00:03:06.865
Then there's things like
continuous integration

00:03:06.865 --> 00:03:09.840
and continuous deployment, that
are whole process around how

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:13.480
you really optimize and automate
the software development life

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:13.980
cycle.

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:16.150
So I think it's really
important to point out,

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:17.740
that when you say
containers, a lot of times,

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:19.710
people think about this,
you know, they kind of see

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:21.084
all these different
pieces, which

00:03:21.084 --> 00:03:22.750
each have specific
areas and names

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:24.775
and technologies behind them.

00:03:24.775 --> 00:03:26.650
But then there's kind
of the actual container

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:29.460
technology that you were
talking about, Craig, as well.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:30.940
So I think it's important
to kind of distinguish

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:32.850
that there's many different
pieces here going on.

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:33.340
CORNELIUS WILLIS:
Well, I mean, we've

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:35.960
talked about almost every part
of software development life

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:37.670
cycle, and this
touches on everything.

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:38.090
KIT COLBERT: It is.

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:39.631
And I think, you
know, to get it back

00:03:39.631 --> 00:03:41.894
to Craig's point,
this notion around how

00:03:41.894 --> 00:03:44.310
every company's kind of becoming
like a web scale internet

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:44.810
company.

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:46.230
I think it's absolutely true.

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:48.510
You know, I think it was
Marc Andreessen, right, four

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:51.310
or five years ago now, that
was saying that, you know,

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:52.560
software's eating the world.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:54.710
It was a big op ed in
the New York Times.

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:57.040
And his point there
was that, company's are

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:59.280
by and large becoming software
companies in the sense

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.520
that software is how they
engage with their users.

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:05.240
And is, by and large, how they
differentiate their products.

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:06.150
Right?

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:07.860
Doesn't matter what
industry you're in,

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:10.030
you're kind of becoming
a software company.

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:11.950
So it's one example
of this, right ?

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:14.660
So this company John Deere,
they blog very publicly

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:17.060
about the kind of
transformation they made.

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:19.570
So this is, again, a company
that does tractors, combines,

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:20.459
that sort of thing.

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:23.120
And yet the value
to their customers

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:25.135
is no longer just the
tractor that they build.

00:04:25.135 --> 00:04:27.480
It's actually the sensors they
put on the tractor, the fact

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:28.515
that it beams that
up to the cloud.

00:04:28.515 --> 00:04:30.230
They have this big
data analytics platform

00:04:30.230 --> 00:04:31.188
that runs in the cloud.

00:04:31.188 --> 00:04:33.880
And that information then goes
back down to the tractors,

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:36.650
so they can then
optimize and maximize

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:37.912
the productivity of the land.

00:04:37.912 --> 00:04:39.620
And that's what they're
really producing.

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:41.940
So think about that transition,
right, as a company.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:44.030
And what John Deere has
been very vocal about

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:46.400
is their use of containers
and continuous integration

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:49.044
and deployment, all these other
things that have come together

00:04:49.044 --> 00:04:51.460
to really differentiate them
as a business in the software

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:51.900
world.

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:53.530
So I think that's
kind of I think if we

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:55.280
ask the why, that's the why.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:58.065
Because if John Deere didn't do
it, someone else would come out

00:04:58.065 --> 00:05:00.969
and they'd make an OK tractor
and an awesome analytics

00:05:00.969 --> 00:05:03.260
platform and they would
eventually beat out John Deere.

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:03.590
Right?

00:05:03.590 --> 00:05:05.830
So I think that's the kind
of competitive environment

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:06.840
that is starting to form.

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:07.850
CORNELIUS WILLIS: Software
is a competitive weapon.

00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:09.310
KIT COLBERT: It is, absolutely.

00:05:09.310 --> 00:05:10.787
It's a huge differentiator.

00:05:10.787 --> 00:05:12.620
CORNELIUS WILLIS: So
myths about containers.

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:15.870
Who's got some myths that we
need to bust about containers?

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:18.742
ALEX POLVI: Definitely
have some myths.

00:05:18.742 --> 00:05:21.200
Like there's a bunch of the
myths rolling around right now.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:24.130
One big one is that containers
replace virtual machines,

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:24.685
for instance.

00:05:24.685 --> 00:05:25.200
I think.

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:28.280
KIT COLBERT: Yeah, that's
one that we hear a lot.

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:29.660
CORNELIUS WILLIS: You agree?

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:31.380
ALEX POLVI: So here's
the big picture.

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:33.230
So container as this
little bit unit,

00:05:33.230 --> 00:05:35.470
yeah, it does kind of
resemble a virtual machine,

00:05:35.470 --> 00:05:36.810
if you like squint
at it the right way.

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:38.440
But when we're talking
about containers,

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:39.630
we're really talking
about this thing

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:41.940
that Craig alluded to, which
is this way of managing

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:43.940
infrastructure that the
hyperscale guys have

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:46.820
done for a while now, but hasn't
been done really in enterprise.

00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:48.640
And the difference there,
is with a virtual machine,

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:49.910
what you do is you
take a big server

00:05:49.910 --> 00:05:51.540
and you carve it up into
lots of little servers

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:53.706
and you treat them as like
their own little servers.

00:05:53.706 --> 00:05:56.321
With containers, you
take a set of containers,

00:05:56.321 --> 00:05:58.820
and you deploy them, and you
manage them as one big machine.

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:00.780
So it allows you to
string a set of machines

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:02.050
as if it was one giant thing.

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:04.680
It's this idea of managing
a data center as a computer,

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:05.359
as one.

00:06:05.359 --> 00:06:07.900
And that has a lot of benefits
around letting the software do

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:10.570
the optimization,
of utilization,

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:13.760
or letting it help the
operations team actually

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:15.550
keep things up and
available, and so on.

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:17.800
So if you think about it
that way, a little bit bigger

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:19.370
picture than just
that container,

00:06:19.370 --> 00:06:23.000
it's kind of
completely different.

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:26.090
One other one, just call out
and let you guys go after that,

00:06:26.090 --> 00:06:27.706
is around security.

00:06:27.706 --> 00:06:30.080
So, containers have gotten a
pretty bad rap for security,

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:31.790
but we have to step
back for a moment

00:06:31.790 --> 00:06:33.560
and think about, what were
you doing before containers.

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:35.550
And what you were doing
before containers,

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:37.966
is you were just running a
bunch of different applications

00:06:37.966 --> 00:06:38.620
on one machine.

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:41.180
And if any of those things
got compromised in any way,

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:43.380
the attacker now can go
get the rest of the server.

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:44.960
And it's rather
trivial from there.

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:46.751
At least with containers,
you're putting up

00:06:46.751 --> 00:06:49.650
some boundaries and walls
between every single individual

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:50.310
application.

00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:52.560
Really, the only way that
you could do that previously

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:54.960
is if you launched a full
server virtual machine

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:56.980
for one application,
which, you know,

00:06:56.980 --> 00:06:59.550
it sometimes happens, but
isn't the like typical use

00:06:59.550 --> 00:07:00.971
case of server infrastructure.

00:07:00.971 --> 00:07:03.470
So even today, the containers
have gotten a lot of criticism

00:07:03.470 --> 00:07:05.580
for security, but
then you just stop

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:07.700
for a second, wait a
minute, before this you

00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:10.872
were just letting everything
kind of go to town, but--

00:07:10.872 --> 00:07:12.830
CRAIG MCLUCKIE: So let
me add to the first one.

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:14.570
And maybe Kitt wants to
talk to the second one.

00:07:14.570 --> 00:07:16.528
Just by-- there's some
interesting conversation

00:07:16.528 --> 00:07:18.310
to be had there.

00:07:18.310 --> 00:07:19.830
I think Alex is exactly right.

00:07:19.830 --> 00:07:21.920
I think the biggest
mistake people make around

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:24.740
containers today is they think
about it the same way that they

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:26.510
think about that
virtual machine.

00:07:26.510 --> 00:07:28.040
And there's a couple
of things that

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:30.040
are really important that
one needs to consider.

00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:31.930
The first is, with
containers you're

00:07:31.930 --> 00:07:35.030
creating something that is
far more automatic and easily

00:07:35.030 --> 00:07:35.690
schedulable.

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:36.680
So if you think about
the way that people

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:38.260
tend to run virtual
machines, you

00:07:38.260 --> 00:07:39.801
build something in
a virtual machine,

00:07:39.801 --> 00:07:41.280
it'd be vertically scalable.

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:42.640
You could add, you know,
additional resources

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:44.050
by creating
additional processes,

00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:46.722
but eventually you'd get to
the boundaries of your system.

00:07:46.722 --> 00:07:48.180
And then the wheels
would fall off.

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:49.670
You would have to
really kind of change

00:07:49.670 --> 00:07:50.910
your sort of way of thinking.

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:53.141
You'd have to make this
big, bold transition

00:07:53.141 --> 00:07:54.640
into a distributed
systems, as well.

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:55.720
It was really hard.

00:07:55.720 --> 00:07:57.470
So it was easy for
people to build systems

00:07:57.470 --> 00:07:58.844
that would scale
to point, but it

00:07:58.844 --> 00:08:01.110
was practically impossible
for most engineers

00:08:01.110 --> 00:08:03.060
to build systems
that got beyond that.

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:04.950
And one of the things
I think that, you know,

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:07.450
we have an opportunity to do,
as we move towards containers,

00:08:07.450 --> 00:08:09.320
is to think about the
item of deployment

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:11.650
as being at the process
level rather than as

00:08:11.650 --> 00:08:15.610
at the sort of subsystem level,
and creating a very natural way

00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:18.094
to tie these things together
in a useful fashion.

00:08:18.094 --> 00:08:20.260
So they can be easily, you
know, stood up, consumed,

00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:22.630
scaled, you know,
turned up, turned down.

00:08:22.630 --> 00:08:25.670
And by doing so, producing
tremendous value for the end

00:08:25.670 --> 00:08:28.055
consumer, because you get
far more efficient use

00:08:28.055 --> 00:08:29.430
of your resources,
you don't have

00:08:29.430 --> 00:08:31.800
to worry about scaling events,
the health of your system

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:34.442
is managed for you, and you
get far more insight into how

00:08:34.442 --> 00:08:36.900
things are behaving because
you're operating at this higher

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:37.399
abstraction.

00:08:37.399 --> 00:08:40.000
I think that's an incredibly
key construct as we

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:41.929
look to the next ten years.

00:08:41.929 --> 00:08:44.865
KIT COLBERT: Yeah, and I'll
say, I think on that note,

00:08:44.865 --> 00:08:46.600
when people think
of VMs, you know,

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:48.490
there's multiple aspects to it.

00:08:48.490 --> 00:08:51.420
I like to just decompose into
two simple aspects of VM.

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:54.340
There's kind of the VM as
the packaging mechanism,

00:08:54.340 --> 00:08:56.120
right, that you
can install in OS

00:08:56.120 --> 00:08:58.480
and put an app in there,
create a VM template, then

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:00.980
provision that thing, you
know, as much as you want.

00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:03.540
And there's also the VM
as the runtime vehicle.

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:06.390
And I think that,
you know, what we

00:09:06.390 --> 00:09:10.871
see is VM packaging was kind of
a necessity before containers

00:09:10.871 --> 00:09:12.870
and just with a lot of
traditional applications,

00:09:12.870 --> 00:09:15.624
especially more off the
shelf applications where

00:09:15.624 --> 00:09:16.540
you didn't control it.

00:09:16.540 --> 00:09:18.690
You didn't have in-house
development able to change it.

00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:20.170
And it was kind
of the way it was.

00:09:20.170 --> 00:09:23.470
And so, VMs offer great
flexibility there.

00:09:23.470 --> 00:09:25.140
But as we get into
the container world,

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:26.510
it makes a lot more
sense, if you can,

00:09:26.510 --> 00:09:28.519
to package an application
inside a container.

00:09:28.519 --> 00:09:30.060
I think our point
of view, of course,

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:32.601
is that we think we can bring
a lot of the benefits of the VM

00:09:32.601 --> 00:09:36.390
runtime to containers, right,
from a runtime scenario.

00:09:36.390 --> 00:09:39.150
But I think the other
point I would make, just

00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:43.840
on the misconceptions
around containers,

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:47.336
is that again this notion that--
when we think of containers,

00:09:47.336 --> 00:09:49.210
again, the association
with that is typically

00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:50.876
like a Google-style
type infrastructure.

00:09:50.876 --> 00:09:52.385
I've got his awesome
app, which is

00:09:52.385 --> 00:09:54.580
like highly
distributed, and totally

00:09:54.580 --> 00:09:56.560
resilient to
infrastructure failure,

00:09:56.560 --> 00:09:58.002
and all these great things.

00:09:58.002 --> 00:10:00.460
And then people say, OK, well,
I can take my old, you know,

00:10:00.460 --> 00:10:02.625
20-year-old, legacy,
monolithic app,

00:10:02.625 --> 00:10:05.000
toss that thing in a container,
and boom, I'm good to go.

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:05.230
Right?

00:10:05.230 --> 00:10:06.320
It's like the magic pixie dust.

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:07.695
And all of a sudden
this thing is

00:10:07.695 --> 00:10:10.466
highly scalable and resilient
to hardware failure.

00:10:10.466 --> 00:10:11.590
And it's like actually, no.

00:10:11.590 --> 00:10:15.091
The container is very helpful,
but if your application--

00:10:15.091 --> 00:10:16.590
it's very helpful
in the sense-- let

00:10:16.590 --> 00:10:18.673
me finish that thought--
very helpful in the sense

00:10:18.673 --> 00:10:20.970
of, you know, being
able to reliably

00:10:20.970 --> 00:10:23.460
and quickly reinstantiate
that application

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:25.300
on any infrastructure you want.

00:10:25.300 --> 00:10:27.670
But if the application
is broken, doesn't scale,

00:10:27.670 --> 00:10:30.592
falls over a lot, they're going
to have a broken, not scalable

00:10:30.592 --> 00:10:33.050
application that falls over a
lot reinstantiated a thousand

00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:34.230
times faster.

00:10:34.230 --> 00:10:35.820
So I think the big
thing that I always

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:39.170
talk to customers about is,
and users about, is like,

00:10:39.170 --> 00:10:41.470
hey, guys, you not only need
to think about containers,

00:10:41.470 --> 00:10:43.450
you also need to think about
application architecture.

00:10:43.450 --> 00:10:44.590
Because that's the
much harder thing

00:10:44.590 --> 00:10:46.810
to do, with how do you
actually re-architect the app

00:10:46.810 --> 00:10:49.750
to take advantage of these, kind
of this distributed paradigm.

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:50.020
CORNELIUS WILLIS: Right.

00:10:50.020 --> 00:10:51.770
It's not a lift and
shift to your old app,

00:10:51.770 --> 00:10:53.450
that suddenly your
old app is magical.

00:10:53.450 --> 00:10:53.850
KIT COLBERT: Yep.

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:55.933
And, again, that's maybe
another VM comment there,

00:10:55.933 --> 00:10:58.260
because the VM enabled that
sort of lift and shift.

00:10:58.260 --> 00:10:59.840
But with containers,
you can do that,

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:01.340
but you don't get all
the benefits, right?

00:11:01.340 --> 00:11:02.840
To really realize
the full benefits,

00:11:02.840 --> 00:11:05.220
you need that sort of rethink
on the architecture side.

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:06.963
CRAIG MCLUCKIE: One
kind of additional point

00:11:06.963 --> 00:11:08.240
to make there is--
I agree with you.

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:10.114
I think there's a big
sort of cognitive shift

00:11:10.114 --> 00:11:12.494
that needs to happen,
but it's far less big

00:11:12.494 --> 00:11:14.410
than the cognitive shift
has ever been before.

00:11:14.410 --> 00:11:15.910
If you think the
bad old days of sOA

00:11:15.910 --> 00:11:18.480
I don't know if either of you
worked on SOA technologies.

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:20.146
ALEX POLVI: Someone
who does XML, right?

00:11:20.146 --> 00:11:21.290
CRAIG MCLUCKIE: Uh, yeah.

00:11:21.290 --> 00:11:24.550
Or tried to deploy an
enterprise service bus.

00:11:24.550 --> 00:11:26.719
The process of actually
getting to the goodness

00:11:26.719 --> 00:11:28.260
of this distributed
systems, you have

00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:30.274
to make these huge investments.

00:11:30.274 --> 00:11:31.940
You'd have to retrain
all your engineers

00:11:31.940 --> 00:11:32.820
around these new patterns.

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:34.653
And you'd have to, you
know, re-architecture

00:11:34.653 --> 00:11:37.200
systems to actually
use specific protocols.

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:39.370
And it was a disaster,
because the energy curve

00:11:39.370 --> 00:11:41.560
was like-- the value
proposition was there.

00:11:41.560 --> 00:11:43.810
It was possible to achieve
these patterns of operating

00:11:43.810 --> 00:11:46.830
previously, but there was such
a cognitive hurdle to get over,

00:11:46.830 --> 00:11:48.210
it was practically impossible.

00:11:48.210 --> 00:11:50.710
And so, I think one of things
that we're starting to see now

00:11:50.710 --> 00:11:52.600
is, you know, Docker
has made it easy to kind

00:11:52.600 --> 00:11:55.110
of encapsulate something in
a hermetically sealed unit.

00:11:55.110 --> 00:11:58.740
Modern sort of technologies
that are actually scheduling it

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:00.540
make it feel like
it's just, you know,

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:02.800
running in a reasonably
local environment.

00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:04.830
But you can use SDN to
wire things together.

00:12:04.830 --> 00:12:07.400
You can use software-defined
storage to actually map volumes

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:09.780
in, create a natural
experience, but actually

00:12:09.780 --> 00:12:12.240
start benefiting from lot
of the values of this higher

00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:13.740
abstraction, which
is very powerful.

00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:15.440
CORNELIUS WILLIS: So it feels
like it's the right time.

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:16.690
KIT COLBERT: Well, I think
that's a really good point.

00:12:16.690 --> 00:12:17.920
I think that's an
excellent point.

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:19.711
Because I think what
you're kind of getting

00:12:19.711 --> 00:12:22.116
to is this whole
notion of, you know,

00:12:22.116 --> 00:12:24.240
the infrastructure architecture
and the application

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:26.520
architecture stack, it
is evolving as well.

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:26.800
CRAIG MCLUCKIE: Yes.

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:28.460
KIT COLBERT: Whereas before, you
had to do a lot of this stuff

00:12:28.460 --> 00:12:31.230
yourself and figure out a lot of
these hard kind of distributed

00:12:31.230 --> 00:12:32.360
systems problems.

00:12:32.360 --> 00:12:35.885
You know, implement your own
Paxos or whatever it is, right?

00:12:35.885 --> 00:12:38.500
What we're seeing now is a lot
of this stuff is available,

00:12:38.500 --> 00:12:40.030
many times open source.

00:12:40.030 --> 00:12:42.094
And so rather than
you figuring it out,

00:12:42.094 --> 00:12:44.010
you can rely on some
other component to do it.

00:12:44.010 --> 00:12:45.754
You can focus much
more quick, I think

00:12:45.754 --> 00:12:47.212
this is your point,
on the business

00:12:47.212 --> 00:12:50.230
logic of whatever the specific
thing is that you're building.

00:12:50.230 --> 00:12:52.270
And not have to worry
about all, like the 10,000

00:12:52.270 --> 00:12:54.371
other kind of problems
that go along with that.

00:12:54.371 --> 00:12:56.120
But you have these
libraries of frameworks

00:12:56.120 --> 00:12:58.342
that you can use out of the box.

00:12:58.342 --> 00:13:00.050
CORNELIUS WILLIS: So
what needs to happen

00:13:00.050 --> 00:13:04.460
in the industry, that isn't
happening now, for containers

00:13:04.460 --> 00:13:05.930
really to take hold?

00:13:05.930 --> 00:13:10.086
What are the hurdles
we need to get over?

00:13:10.086 --> 00:13:10.924
ALEX POLVI: Here.

00:13:10.924 --> 00:13:12.590
Here's where we're
at right now with it.

00:13:12.590 --> 00:13:15.100
It's this whole
new platform, OK?

00:13:15.100 --> 00:13:16.990
And I like to use the
analogy of a phone.

00:13:16.990 --> 00:13:17.530
OK?

00:13:17.530 --> 00:13:21.050
Imagine if your iPhone
or Android booted up,

00:13:21.050 --> 00:13:22.952
and there was no apps on it.

00:13:22.952 --> 00:13:24.910
And the first thing you
had to do as a business

00:13:24.910 --> 00:13:26.993
was to write an application
to make the platforms.

00:13:26.993 --> 00:13:30.019
There's revolutionary new
platform, paradigm shift,

00:13:30.019 --> 00:13:32.560
everything, but the very first
thing you have to do to use it

00:13:32.560 --> 00:13:36.199
is to create your own unit
of value on top of it.

00:13:36.199 --> 00:13:38.240
And that's where we're at
right now. [INAUDIBLE].

00:13:38.240 --> 00:13:41.719
All the tools are there, but
the teams that are using them

00:13:41.719 --> 00:13:43.260
have to do a lot of
the heavy lifting

00:13:43.260 --> 00:13:45.612
to sort of feel the value of it.

00:13:45.612 --> 00:13:47.070
So I think, as an
industry, we have

00:13:47.070 --> 00:13:49.440
to mature a little
bit in terms of having

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:52.344
solutions put together for
users that already go in there.

00:13:52.344 --> 00:13:53.510
We need to write the dialer.

00:13:53.510 --> 00:13:54.843
We need to write the camera app.

00:13:54.843 --> 00:13:56.800
We need to write the
gallery app for you.

00:13:56.800 --> 00:13:58.591
And that kind of stuff,
the general purpose

00:13:58.591 --> 00:14:00.500
things that every
operations team needs just

00:14:00.500 --> 00:14:03.225
to make the thing useful
to them is the challenges

00:14:03.225 --> 00:14:05.100
that I think we're
getting through right now.

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:07.380
But the base platform,
it's getting pretty good.

00:14:07.380 --> 00:14:08.335
And that's exciting.

00:14:08.335 --> 00:14:09.020
CRAIG MCLUCKIE: I
agree with that.

00:14:09.020 --> 00:14:10.561
I would add one
thing, which is thing

00:14:10.561 --> 00:14:13.700
is really important
is, we have to create

00:14:13.700 --> 00:14:15.877
effective standards around
some of the core pieces

00:14:15.877 --> 00:14:17.210
that everyone wants to build on.

00:14:17.210 --> 00:14:19.426
If you think what
containers are doing

00:14:19.426 --> 00:14:20.800
is they're creating
a natural way

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:22.650
to express a piece of
software and describe

00:14:22.650 --> 00:14:24.202
how you want it
run, and creating

00:14:24.202 --> 00:14:26.660
a framework so you can actually
take that thing and run it.

00:14:26.660 --> 00:14:28.350
And we need to create
a standard that

00:14:28.350 --> 00:14:31.770
lets people really
innovate and advance

00:14:31.770 --> 00:14:35.180
the story around the packaging
and distribution of containers,

00:14:35.180 --> 00:14:36.920
continuous integration
and delivery,

00:14:36.920 --> 00:14:40.380
new and unique ways to, you
know, to take code and actually

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:43.310
get into this environment,
secure operating

00:14:43.310 --> 00:14:46.200
environments for these And we
need to do that in a way that

00:14:46.200 --> 00:14:47.429
fosters a whole ecosystem.

00:14:47.429 --> 00:14:49.220
And to do that, you
need to actually define

00:14:49.220 --> 00:14:49.928
a clean standard.

00:14:49.928 --> 00:14:51.860
And then on the
other side of it,

00:14:51.860 --> 00:14:54.110
you need to be able to
consume that and run it

00:14:54.110 --> 00:14:56.150
in a broad array
of technologies.

00:14:56.150 --> 00:14:58.380
I mean, each of us
are vendors and have

00:14:58.380 --> 00:15:00.890
our own set of offerings
that we need to consume.

00:15:00.890 --> 00:15:04.756
It'd be kind of nice if there
was a standard that people

00:15:04.756 --> 00:15:06.630
could really invest in,
that really decoupled

00:15:06.630 --> 00:15:09.260
the production, the
consumption of technology.

00:15:09.260 --> 00:15:11.120
And that's why I
think things like OCI

00:15:11.120 --> 00:15:12.490
are incredibly important.

00:15:12.490 --> 00:15:14.939
It's incredibly important
that OCI focus not just on--

00:15:14.939 --> 00:15:15.730
KIT COLBERT: Sorry.

00:15:15.730 --> 00:15:16.850
OCI, Open Container Initiative.

00:15:16.850 --> 00:15:19.058
CRAIG MCLUCKIE: Open Container
Initiative, thank you.

00:15:19.058 --> 00:15:23.030
Focus on really creating a
clean and sort of well adopted

00:15:23.030 --> 00:15:27.260
standard that separates the
production and distribution

00:15:27.260 --> 00:15:30.100
of container technologies from
the runtime and consumption

00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:30.729
of it.

00:15:30.729 --> 00:15:32.270
CORNELIUS WILLIS:
But standardization

00:15:32.270 --> 00:15:33.640
on both sides of that?

00:15:33.640 --> 00:15:34.515
CRAIG MCLUCKIE: Yeah.

00:15:34.515 --> 00:15:37.100
So having a standard
sort of specification

00:15:37.100 --> 00:15:39.220
so that the people who're
operating on the one

00:15:39.220 --> 00:15:40.678
side of the divide
to actually want

00:15:40.678 --> 00:15:42.941
to build and [INAUDIBLE]
containers can, you know,

00:15:42.941 --> 00:15:44.065
work to that specification.

00:15:44.065 --> 00:15:45.740
And then people
on the other side

00:15:45.740 --> 00:15:47.740
of the divide, who want
to take those containers

00:15:47.740 --> 00:15:50.961
and run them and operate them,
have a common ground to meet.

00:15:50.961 --> 00:15:52.960
So that the tool providers
aren't trying to tool

00:15:52.960 --> 00:15:55.780
for n different
providers' implementations

00:15:55.780 --> 00:15:58.360
of the specification.

00:15:58.360 --> 00:16:00.590
But that it also allows
each of the n providers

00:16:00.590 --> 00:16:02.425
and specification to
create unique value

00:16:02.425 --> 00:16:04.815
and differentiate
and actually offer up

00:16:04.815 --> 00:16:06.310
real innovation in that space.

00:16:06.310 --> 00:16:07.060
KIT COLBERT: Yeah.

00:16:07.060 --> 00:16:10.400
And, you know, getting back to
the question around adoption

00:16:10.400 --> 00:16:12.240
and, you know, the
maturity and the space.

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:14.800
So I think if you look at
what happened, let's say,

00:16:14.800 --> 00:16:17.160
with VMware story and
the virtualization space.

00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:19.015
I mean, that took a
good, I don't know,

00:16:19.015 --> 00:16:21.500
let's say-- really started
this on the data center

00:16:21.500 --> 00:16:23.051
started probably in 2000, 2001.

00:16:23.051 --> 00:16:24.550
And just in the
last couple of years

00:16:24.550 --> 00:16:25.924
have we reached
kind of the point

00:16:25.924 --> 00:16:28.764
where people aren't afraid
to virtualize things anymore.

00:16:28.764 --> 00:16:30.680
Before there was always
some kind of concerns.

00:16:30.680 --> 00:16:33.180
And the concerns varied in
a number of different ways.

00:16:33.180 --> 00:16:36.190
A lot of times it was just a
question of, you know, can I,

00:16:36.190 --> 00:16:38.710
as an operations group, really
run this thing in production

00:16:38.710 --> 00:16:40.640
with some sort of
assurance that it will run,

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:43.230
and, you know, I can take care
of problems when they happen,

00:16:43.230 --> 00:16:44.030
and so forth.

00:16:44.030 --> 00:16:46.820
And so I think what we've
seen is tremendous excitement

00:16:46.820 --> 00:16:48.820
from developers because
developers get the value

00:16:48.820 --> 00:16:49.940
proposition of containers.

00:16:49.940 --> 00:16:52.610
You know, they get Docker and
they see how it can help them.

00:16:52.610 --> 00:16:55.089
And so now I think that the
transition point is-- well,

00:16:55.089 --> 00:16:57.630
sorry to fall on Craig's point
that we're seeing tooling come

00:16:57.630 --> 00:17:01.050
out around this as well
throughout the CICD space,

00:17:01.050 --> 00:17:02.750
and now over onto
the production side.

00:17:02.750 --> 00:17:04.791
I think that's going to
be where the rubber meets

00:17:04.791 --> 00:17:07.754
the road in terms of like,
as IT teams and operations

00:17:07.754 --> 00:17:09.170
teams start to
take a look at this

00:17:09.170 --> 00:17:11.079
and say, OK, how do we actually
run these things in production.

00:17:11.079 --> 00:17:13.730
How do I make sure it's secure,
and how do I audit that,

00:17:13.730 --> 00:17:15.960
and how do I monitor it,
and do I need to do backups?

00:17:15.960 --> 00:17:18.324
You know, all the sort
of things that they

00:17:18.324 --> 00:17:19.240
have to think through.

00:17:19.240 --> 00:17:21.986
So I think what we're seeing
is-- I think this has actually

00:17:21.986 --> 00:17:24.194
kind of evolved faster,
probably, than virtualization

00:17:24.194 --> 00:17:26.819
did, just because the amount of
interest that's here right now,

00:17:26.819 --> 00:17:29.750
and the amount of folks that
are jumping into the space.

00:17:29.750 --> 00:17:33.729
But at the same time, I think
that right now it's very much,

00:17:33.729 --> 00:17:35.270
for most of the
customers we talk to,

00:17:35.270 --> 00:17:36.820
they're the kind of
cutting edge ones,

00:17:36.820 --> 00:17:39.290
the ones who are willing to take
a bunch of this stuff that's

00:17:39.290 --> 00:17:41.539
still kind of, you know,
just starting out and kind of

00:17:41.539 --> 00:17:43.690
put it together in a
way that works for them.

00:17:43.690 --> 00:17:45.414
And what you'll see
happening over time

00:17:45.414 --> 00:17:47.080
is more out of the
box solutions, things

00:17:47.080 --> 00:17:49.730
that just kind of work,
and are able to let

00:17:49.730 --> 00:17:51.289
these IT teams take it and run.

00:17:51.289 --> 00:17:53.580
CORNELIUS WILLIS: So what's
happening with Cloud-Native

00:17:53.580 --> 00:17:54.820
Computing Foundation?

00:17:54.820 --> 00:17:56.830
It seems kind of quiet lately.

00:17:56.830 --> 00:17:57.639
What's the story?

00:17:57.639 --> 00:17:58.430
CRAIG MCLUCKIE: OK.

00:17:58.430 --> 00:18:00.730
I'm glad you asked the question.

00:18:00.730 --> 00:18:02.730
So we've been
doing a lot of work

00:18:02.730 --> 00:18:05.260
to basically put
together a framework

00:18:05.260 --> 00:18:08.590
that we think will work
well for the community.

00:18:08.590 --> 00:18:10.670
We're looking to really
create a foundation that

00:18:10.670 --> 00:18:14.310
operates fairly, that has a
very strong technical identity

00:18:14.310 --> 00:18:16.600
and technical
voice, that welcomes

00:18:16.600 --> 00:18:19.960
a broad array of technologies,
but at the same time achieves

00:18:19.960 --> 00:18:21.984
a convergence that
makes it useful.

00:18:21.984 --> 00:18:23.400
So we've been
spending quite a lot

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:25.500
of time in a series
of conversations

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:27.740
through the community
channels to really get

00:18:27.740 --> 00:18:29.710
the details of how
to make it run work.

00:18:29.710 --> 00:18:32.420
And there's actually a lot of
effort and a lot of thought

00:18:32.420 --> 00:18:34.240
is going into, you
know, how do we

00:18:34.240 --> 00:18:36.970
create an effective
oversight committee that

00:18:36.970 --> 00:18:40.390
isn't bound to a specific
commercial interest.

00:18:40.390 --> 00:18:42.790
How do we create
a group of people

00:18:42.790 --> 00:18:45.900
that are representing the end
users so that that voice isn't

00:18:45.900 --> 00:18:49.090
drowned out by vendors that have
a strong commercial interest?

00:18:49.090 --> 00:18:50.790
And then, you know,
how do we create

00:18:50.790 --> 00:18:54.990
a principled architecture that
is, you know, fair to everyone

00:18:54.990 --> 00:18:57.859
in the community, whether
they're end users and vendors,

00:18:57.859 --> 00:18:59.400
and really sort of
bring it together.

00:18:59.400 --> 00:19:01.610
So behind the scenes
we've been working really

00:19:01.610 --> 00:19:05.235
hard to get a lot of
the details ironed out

00:19:05.235 --> 00:19:06.860
about how it's going
to work, you know,

00:19:06.860 --> 00:19:09.930
how things will be managed,
what the governing structure is,

00:19:09.930 --> 00:19:12.650
and we're getting very
close to actually being

00:19:12.650 --> 00:19:15.170
able to make a really, you know,
sort of strong announcement

00:19:15.170 --> 00:19:17.412
around, you know, getting
through contracting

00:19:17.412 --> 00:19:19.370
and actually starting
the day-to-day operations

00:19:19.370 --> 00:19:20.119
of the foundation.

00:19:20.119 --> 00:19:21.550
So things are going very well.

00:19:21.550 --> 00:19:23.091
CORNELIUS WILLIS:
So assuming that we

00:19:23.091 --> 00:19:24.670
have a successful
standards efforts,

00:19:24.670 --> 00:19:26.700
and we're able to
link the development

00:19:26.700 --> 00:19:30.420
world with the runtime
world through well-expressed

00:19:30.420 --> 00:19:32.820
standards, what does a
thriving tool ecosystem

00:19:32.820 --> 00:19:34.640
look like for containers?

00:19:34.640 --> 00:19:37.280
ALEX POLVI: Sure, so I like
the standard shipping container

00:19:37.280 --> 00:19:40.680
analogy a lot for this,
because it's very applicable.

00:19:40.680 --> 00:19:43.040
So once we have our
standard on the unit,

00:19:43.040 --> 00:19:45.890
you know, the goal is not
just to have a standard.

00:19:45.890 --> 00:19:47.380
It's to have like
a global economy.

00:19:47.380 --> 00:19:48.520
You know?

00:19:48.520 --> 00:19:49.940
That's the point of this.

00:19:49.940 --> 00:19:53.184
So by having a unit
that, as a user,

00:19:53.184 --> 00:19:55.100
you can package against
and know that you have

00:19:55.100 --> 00:19:57.850
a variety of vendors that
all interoperate with that,

00:19:57.850 --> 00:20:01.370
you should see a rich
ecosystem of people

00:20:01.370 --> 00:20:04.130
that are competing
for your business,

00:20:04.130 --> 00:20:07.070
as well as some pretty good
innovations because of that.

00:20:07.070 --> 00:20:09.410
If we have the bare minimum
piece of a standard, that

00:20:09.410 --> 00:20:11.660
means, as vendors, we all
have to duke it out and like

00:20:11.660 --> 00:20:12.270
earn your business.

00:20:12.270 --> 00:20:12.440
Right?

00:20:12.440 --> 00:20:14.930
We have to make our stuff
faster, more secure, and more

00:20:14.930 --> 00:20:16.160
reliable, and so on.

00:20:16.160 --> 00:20:18.780
So for the end user, the
standards create that.

00:20:18.780 --> 00:20:20.569
And, again, using
the analogy here,

00:20:20.569 --> 00:20:21.985
the goal is not
the standard, it's

00:20:21.985 --> 00:20:25.270
the whole global economy
that's created because there's

00:20:25.270 --> 00:20:26.685
that basic standard in place.

00:20:26.685 --> 00:20:27.435
KIT COLBERT: Yeah.

00:20:27.435 --> 00:20:29.730
I think Alex, you know,
hit on that with this--

00:20:29.730 --> 00:20:33.530
and what we talk about when we
talk about this with customers,

00:20:33.530 --> 00:20:36.201
the word that I hear a
lot to is optionality.

00:20:36.201 --> 00:20:36.700
Right?

00:20:36.700 --> 00:20:39.060
This ability to not get
locked into something,

00:20:39.060 --> 00:20:43.480
that they can make choices
based on their requirements,

00:20:43.480 --> 00:20:46.260
they can-- free to change
those choices as they need to.

00:20:46.260 --> 00:20:47.815
And I think that's
kind of the vision

00:20:47.815 --> 00:20:50.340
that we're all trying to get
to, where-- and by the way,

00:20:50.340 --> 00:20:52.410
that's also a big
reason for open source.

00:20:52.410 --> 00:20:54.160
We have a lot of open
source technologies.

00:20:54.160 --> 00:20:56.240
And customers wanted to
leverage those things.

00:20:56.240 --> 00:20:57.740
It, again, goes
back to optionality,

00:20:57.740 --> 00:21:01.057
and multiple vendors backing
that open source project.

00:21:01.057 --> 00:21:03.390
So I think, you know, when
we see the container space as

00:21:03.390 --> 00:21:05.848
well, we don't want it to become
this super fragmented area

00:21:05.848 --> 00:21:08.620
where, OK-- if that
happens, you'll

00:21:08.620 --> 00:21:11.287
start making these crazy kind of
chains of dependency decisions,

00:21:11.287 --> 00:21:12.953
like OK, so if I
select this technology,

00:21:12.953 --> 00:21:15.510
then I can do that thing well,
this other thing not so well.

00:21:15.510 --> 00:21:17.470
If I do this one, and
that one works OK.

00:21:17.470 --> 00:21:18.220
And it's like, no.

00:21:18.220 --> 00:21:19.610
Like let's make it very simple.

00:21:19.610 --> 00:21:23.207
You do the default, standard
packaging, or whatever it is,

00:21:23.207 --> 00:21:24.290
and then you have choices.

00:21:24.290 --> 00:21:26.600
Do I want to use this sort
of technology for networking?

00:21:26.600 --> 00:21:27.430
This one for storage?

00:21:27.430 --> 00:21:28.340
This one for scheduling?

00:21:28.340 --> 00:21:29.298
This one for consensus?

00:21:29.298 --> 00:21:31.540
You know, it gives
you options and allows

00:21:31.540 --> 00:21:33.105
you to leverage
the best of breed,

00:21:33.105 --> 00:21:35.230
without getting locked into
anything down the road.

00:21:35.230 --> 00:21:35.980
CORNELIUS WILLIS:
On the vendor side,

00:21:35.980 --> 00:21:37.610
it also lets you
invest in the standard,

00:21:37.610 --> 00:21:39.030
so you can innovate
off the standards--

00:21:39.030 --> 00:21:39.340
KIT COLBERT: Exactly.

00:21:39.340 --> 00:21:40.798
We make our solutions
better rather

00:21:40.798 --> 00:21:43.200
than having five people
reinvent the wheel six times.

00:21:43.200 --> 00:21:45.710
CRAIG MCLUCKIE: And I think
that actually is a key element

00:21:45.710 --> 00:21:46.664
to driving innovation.

00:21:46.664 --> 00:21:48.580
And to extend that to
other parts of the stack

00:21:48.580 --> 00:21:50.580
as well, I think it
makes sense to try

00:21:50.580 --> 00:21:54.690
to create a series of principled
interfaces between subsystems

00:21:54.690 --> 00:21:59.770
so that if someone has a
really killer implementation

00:21:59.770 --> 00:22:01.849
of something, they can
safely invest in that,

00:22:01.849 --> 00:22:03.390
and innovate in it,
and actually have

00:22:03.390 --> 00:22:05.650
it pulled through into a
variety of other areas.

00:22:05.650 --> 00:22:08.150
And so I think, you know, really
pursuing that is important.

00:22:08.150 --> 00:22:10.070
And I think, you know,
one thing I'd like to say,

00:22:10.070 --> 00:22:12.111
which is kind of just
stepping back a little bit,

00:22:12.111 --> 00:22:15.710
when I look to the future
here, a lot of things

00:22:15.710 --> 00:22:16.650
are going to change.

00:22:16.650 --> 00:22:20.340
And people try to play their
understanding of how operations

00:22:20.340 --> 00:22:22.860
works into this new world.

00:22:22.860 --> 00:22:24.210
And it's going to change.

00:22:24.210 --> 00:22:26.658
We're going to create a
radical specialization of ops.

00:22:26.658 --> 00:22:28.033
The number of
people you're going

00:22:28.033 --> 00:22:31.170
to have to maintain to
operate a series of systems

00:22:31.170 --> 00:22:33.150
is going to reduce, and
specialized operations

00:22:33.150 --> 00:22:33.930
function is going to emerge.

00:22:33.930 --> 00:22:36.160
And I expect each of
those operations functions

00:22:36.160 --> 00:22:37.970
will have a unique set of tools.

00:22:37.970 --> 00:22:39.650
And it should be
safe for providers

00:22:39.650 --> 00:22:42.570
to really focus on
lighting those things up.

00:22:42.570 --> 00:22:45.644
The container abstraction level
provides a remarkable amount

00:22:45.644 --> 00:22:48.060
of data you could use for
things like intrusion detection,

00:22:48.060 --> 00:22:48.920
if you want to drive security.

00:22:48.920 --> 00:22:51.340
It'd be wonderful if an
ecosystem could emerge around

00:22:51.340 --> 00:22:52.460
that.

00:22:52.460 --> 00:22:54.060
So I think that's
likely to happen.

00:22:54.060 --> 00:22:55.200
And then the other thing
I think is going to happen

00:22:55.200 --> 00:22:58.060
is, you know, one of the things
we saw inside Google when

00:22:58.060 --> 00:23:00.060
we made this transition
a long time ago is

00:23:00.060 --> 00:23:02.840
this incredible explosion
in productivity.

00:23:02.840 --> 00:23:05.410
Turns out, when you actually
make this transition to not

00:23:05.410 --> 00:23:07.535
having to have your engineers
spend half their life

00:23:07.535 --> 00:23:10.045
or more actually trying
to maintain and operate

00:23:10.045 --> 00:23:13.112
the thing they just built-- but
that principled systems can run

00:23:13.112 --> 00:23:15.070
them for them-- like they
become radically more

00:23:15.070 --> 00:23:16.570
productive, radically
more code gets

00:23:16.570 --> 00:23:19.980
produced, and you need new
classes of tools, and new ways

00:23:19.980 --> 00:23:22.920
to actually define policy,
and see what's happening,

00:23:22.920 --> 00:23:24.900
and track the things
that are being deployed.

00:23:24.900 --> 00:23:28.147
So I think it's going
to introduce a very

00:23:28.147 --> 00:23:30.230
substantive change in the
way that people actually

00:23:30.230 --> 00:23:33.807
build and operate and
run these technologies.

00:23:33.807 --> 00:23:35.890
And the actual set of
tools-- the suite of tools--

00:23:35.890 --> 00:23:38.402
is going to evolve in
new and interesting ways.

00:23:38.402 --> 00:23:42.660
CORNELIUS WILLIS: So what's
the preflight checklist?

00:23:42.660 --> 00:23:43.170
Right?

00:23:43.170 --> 00:23:45.870
If we look at, at
some point this

00:23:45.870 --> 00:23:48.370
is going to become-- container
technology is going to become

00:23:48.370 --> 00:23:50.520
the default packaging
mechanism, the default way

00:23:50.520 --> 00:23:51.560
we develop software.

00:23:51.560 --> 00:23:55.690
What's the preflight checklist
for how that happens?

00:23:55.690 --> 00:23:58.230
When do we know
that we're there?

00:23:58.230 --> 00:23:59.290
ALEX POLVI: Well.

00:23:59.290 --> 00:24:01.180
I have an answer to that one.

00:24:01.180 --> 00:24:03.370
I think, so, it's
really about the user.

00:24:03.370 --> 00:24:06.850
And when the user is able to
package their application once,

00:24:06.850 --> 00:24:09.860
sign it-- which means it's them
saying this is my package--

00:24:09.860 --> 00:24:11.830
and then run it across
a variety of vendors--

00:24:11.830 --> 00:24:13.710
they can choose Google
Container Engine,

00:24:13.710 --> 00:24:16.080
they can choose the
VMware products,

00:24:16.080 --> 00:24:17.850
they can choose
Tectonic from CoreOS,

00:24:17.850 --> 00:24:20.150
like these different things
or any other vendor that

00:24:20.150 --> 00:24:21.900
wants to interoperate
with that standard--

00:24:21.900 --> 00:24:23.290
but the user can take that unit.

00:24:23.290 --> 00:24:25.150
He can say, this is mine.

00:24:25.150 --> 00:24:28.900
And then they can run
it in their own place

00:24:28.900 --> 00:24:30.254
of their choosing.

00:24:30.254 --> 00:24:31.670
I think that's
when we've done it,

00:24:31.670 --> 00:24:33.320
we actually have
interoperability,

00:24:33.320 --> 00:24:35.372
and standardization,
and choice for users.

00:24:35.372 --> 00:24:36.830
KIT COLBERT: And
I think it's going

00:24:36.830 --> 00:24:39.410
to take the classic sort
of adoption curve, right?

00:24:39.410 --> 00:24:41.265
Where you're going to have some
people on the leading edge who

00:24:41.265 --> 00:24:43.015
are willing to like
roll up their sleeves,

00:24:43.015 --> 00:24:45.630
and do the extra work, and
sort through the myriad number

00:24:45.630 --> 00:24:48.430
of options, and just
make it work, right?

00:24:48.430 --> 00:24:51.376
And deal with some of
the issues of the changes

00:24:51.376 --> 00:24:53.000
and so forth that
happen down the road.

00:24:53.000 --> 00:24:53.506
But I think we're
definitely going

00:24:53.506 --> 00:24:55.790
to hit an inflection
point where it is

00:24:55.790 --> 00:24:57.100
kind of ready for mass market.

00:24:57.100 --> 00:24:59.080
And that sort of the
point that Alex is making.

00:24:59.080 --> 00:25:01.580
Is that there's going to be a
point at which the stuff works

00:25:01.580 --> 00:25:04.270
well enough out of the
box, the early adopters

00:25:04.270 --> 00:25:06.660
have figured out a lot
the hard problems around

00:25:06.660 --> 00:25:09.193
operationalizing these things
and production, and some

00:25:09.193 --> 00:25:10.290
of the other issues there.

00:25:10.290 --> 00:25:12.040
And then-- we're
always trying to do--

00:25:12.040 --> 00:25:14.016
the cool about the
container space

00:25:14.016 --> 00:25:15.390
is that it really
is a community.

00:25:15.390 --> 00:25:17.840
If you go to any of like the
meet ups, or the conferences,

00:25:17.840 --> 00:25:20.080
you see a lot of people
sharing their experiences.

00:25:20.080 --> 00:25:23.255
Like here is, you know-- at
a company, I took this thing,

00:25:23.255 --> 00:25:25.630
we started running it, and
here's all the problems I hit,

00:25:25.630 --> 00:25:27.490
and here's how to avoid
those in the future.

00:25:27.490 --> 00:25:30.130
So as we collect that sort
of knowledge as an industry

00:25:30.130 --> 00:25:32.240
and as a community,
it will help everyone

00:25:32.240 --> 00:25:34.074
who's new to that
community even go faster.

00:25:34.074 --> 00:25:35.740
CRAIG MCLUCKIE: Yeah,
I agree with that.

00:25:35.740 --> 00:25:37.930
I think every customer's
going to be different.

00:25:37.930 --> 00:25:39.940
We already have a lot
of customers that are,

00:25:39.940 --> 00:25:43.760
you know, they roll out these
amazing statistics around how

00:25:43.760 --> 00:25:45.220
it's changed the
way that they take

00:25:45.220 --> 00:25:47.270
the technology to production.

00:25:47.270 --> 00:25:50.030
And they're able to go from
one roll out every two weeks

00:25:50.030 --> 00:25:53.527
to multiple roll outs a day,
that their operations are

00:25:53.527 --> 00:25:54.360
sort of [INAUDIBLE].

00:25:54.360 --> 00:25:56.700
So for a lot of the
fore leading people,

00:25:56.700 --> 00:25:58.270
it's been ready
for quite a while,

00:25:58.270 --> 00:26:00.390
and they're already
benefiting from it.

00:26:00.390 --> 00:26:02.610
At the far end of the
spectrum, a lot of folks

00:26:02.610 --> 00:26:04.589
need developer
training to actually

00:26:04.589 --> 00:26:05.880
become familiar with the tools.

00:26:05.880 --> 00:26:08.820
They need their favorite systems
integrated to actually develop

00:26:08.820 --> 00:26:11.200
a practice around this and
that, to actually fully

00:26:11.200 --> 00:26:13.260
inculcate and acquire a
staff that can actually

00:26:13.260 --> 00:26:14.560
support their needs.

00:26:14.560 --> 00:26:17.900
And so, it's going to trickle
through technology exactly

00:26:17.900 --> 00:26:18.580
as VMs did.

00:26:18.580 --> 00:26:19.080
Right?

00:26:19.080 --> 00:26:21.610
Like if you think about
that VM adoption cycle.

00:26:21.610 --> 00:26:24.240
It took eight years
or however long

00:26:24.240 --> 00:26:25.466
to really become prevalent.

00:26:25.466 --> 00:26:26.840
I think it'll
probably go quicker

00:26:26.840 --> 00:26:29.500
because, to Kitt's
point, there's

00:26:29.500 --> 00:26:31.125
a lot of interest around it.

00:26:31.125 --> 00:26:33.500
But I think every customer
will make their decision based

00:26:33.500 --> 00:26:37.471
on their specific needs around
completeness of tooling,

00:26:37.471 --> 00:26:38.970
willingness to
engage in open source

00:26:38.970 --> 00:26:41.470
and actually participate in the
evolution of the open source

00:26:41.470 --> 00:26:45.150
technology, their
vendor providing them

00:26:45.150 --> 00:26:47.149
a comprehensive suite
of tools that they need,

00:26:47.149 --> 00:26:49.440
their systems integration
partners actually being there

00:26:49.440 --> 00:26:52.610
to help them through some
of the transition processes.

00:26:52.610 --> 00:26:56.030
So I don't think there's
a one phase answer,

00:26:56.030 --> 00:26:58.010
but it's going a lot
faster that I expected,

00:26:58.010 --> 00:27:00.130
is what I would say.

00:27:00.130 --> 00:27:01.640
CORNELIUS WILLIS:
So with that, I

00:27:01.640 --> 00:27:04.950
think we've had a good, grown up
conversation about containers.

00:27:04.950 --> 00:27:06.620
No chairs were thrown.

00:27:06.620 --> 00:27:08.100
Nobody stalked off the set.

00:27:08.100 --> 00:27:08.735
That's good.

00:27:08.735 --> 00:27:09.610
KIT COLBERT: Success.

00:27:09.610 --> 00:27:10.693
CORNELIUS WILLIS: Success.

00:27:10.693 --> 00:27:13.780
So thank you all for
joining us today.

00:27:13.780 --> 00:27:15.840
And thank you for watching.

00:27:15.840 --> 00:27:19.650
As I think you can hear,
this is the coming framework

00:27:19.650 --> 00:27:22.020
for how we will write
and run software.

00:27:22.020 --> 00:27:25.430
So our encouragement to you is
to go out and learn about it.

00:27:25.430 --> 00:27:27.520
And there are lots of
resources, from all three

00:27:27.520 --> 00:27:29.830
of these vendors, about
container technology.

00:27:29.830 --> 00:27:31.380
So go try it out.

00:27:31.380 --> 00:27:32.470
Go work with it.

00:27:32.470 --> 00:27:34.910
Thanks so much for joining us.

